This version of lharc is a tuned version. Try, if it runsfaster than yours. The changes are computer science nitty-gritty, soi don't want to post them, since they are of minor interest.The changes are:inline functions in lzhuf.cchanging int to short, where possibleusing a 16k IO-Bufferresult >30% more speed on my machineKnut Grunwald, Maximilianstr.25, 6729 Jockgrim, BRDThere has been more changes:1. The time is now set. The unpacked files have the time stamp they had at	packing time2. The attributes are saved, if not disabled with the g (generic) option.	This allows to save execution directories without attribute problems.    The -g option allows you to create ST and PC compatible archives under    OS9. This archives are unpackable with older versions of OS9 lharc.3. There is a Unix compatible Version now. This one saves attributes and	group/user id. The normal Version is called lharcs for lharc strict. 	The one with user id is called lharc. The archives created with the	"normal" lharc should not be unpacked with earlier versions of lharc,    since the attributes of the unpacked file will become garbage. This    is true for PC's and ST's too. This is also the reason for lharcs,    just to allow you to create archives for machines with not full    powered versions of lharc.	A tree saved with this version will restore to the original, except	for the creation date.4. The unpacking part uses _ss_size to get enough room before trying to	unpack a file.5. The test output, if you test archives is now directed to "/nil" and not	to /dev/null.This reflects the 14.12.91 Version.It includes the changes made by S. Paschedag send to comp.os.os9.This archive is packed with the strict version, so set the executablebits of lharc and lharcs.For the compilation:I use Gnu-C. The used DEFS are in DEFS_SYS, the used lib is in LIB. Theos9lib of TOPS may be used, but "NO WARRANTY". It should run, since afriend of me has made the compilation and was able to pack and unpackarchives.The makefile.gcc is for Gnu-C, makefile.cc_osk for Microware C.makefile contains a version, which uses parts of blarslib and parts ofos9lib. makefile.strict creates the strict version, with GCC.In order to make "lharc":makdir RELSmake -f=makefile.gccdel rels/*                              "just do it"make -f=makefile.strictThis procedure should change, but i don't have the time to fix it. May besomeone posts a working makefile to the net.P.S. A few days after the post i had to recognize, that the "UNIX-compatible"     archives cannot be unpacked by UNIX-lharc. I don't know why !!     Since it works fine for OSK and restoring of group/user and time is     very useful, I posted the same version packed with a "lharcs ag ...",     which i wanted to post the first time anyway (Just a mistake in packing).     If someone fixed the bug please send a diff to:     s_grunwa@irau30.ira.uka.de or (were 30 may be replaced by 26-31)     s_grunwald@iravcl.ira.uka.de